"""Original storage, caching, scaling, and pipeline lessons."""
from teaching.foundations import put, LESSONS
put(9, '''Storage choices begin with promises: what is accepted, when it is committed, how it can be read, and which failures it survives. A chat history, an audit ledger, and a thumbnail cache need different retention and correctness contracts. Name the authoritative copy and recovery procedure. A database label alone does not establish transactions, isolation, backups, or acknowledgment durability; these depend on configuration and usage. Choose by access patterns and invariants before comparing product features. || Identify authoritative records / Define commit and read contract / State tolerated failures / Test recovery and retention

A relational model represents facts as relations with keys and constraints; SQL expresses queries and changes over that model. Foreign keys and transactions can protect relationships when configured and used appropriately. A ledger benefits from explicit account and entry relationships and atomic updates. Joins are not inherently a scaling failure; indexes, cardinality, workload, and execution plans matter. Start normalized for ownership and invariants, then denormalize specific read paths when evidence justifies maintenance cost. || Accounts keyed by ID / Entries reference accounts / Transaction protects related changes / Query plan determines access cost

A document database groups nested data under a document identity and commonly permits flexible structures. A user profile with preferences may fit a document aggregate well. Flexibility moves some validation and migration responsibility into schema conventions and application code; it does not make schema disappear. Decide whether updates across documents need atomicity, how nested collections grow, and which fields are indexed. An unbounded array of all chat messages in one user document is usually a poor ownership and growth boundary. || Profile document owns preferences / Validate supported document version / Bound nested collection growth / Cross-document rules need explicit guarantees

A key-value store maps keys to values under a defined read/write contract. It suits sessions, cached objects, or direct ID lookup when operations match the key design. Querying arbitrary value fields may require secondary structures maintained separately. Do not assume all key-value stores share durability or consistency behavior. A key such as tenant:42:session:7 should encode the required identity scope, while expiration and invalidation determine whether the value remains usable after permissions change. || Tenant-scoped key / Direct value lookup / Expiration or replacement / Secondary query needs another access path

Wide-column systems organize data around partition keys and ordered clustering dimensions in common implementations. A conversation partition with messages ordered by sequence can support bounded history reads. Partition size and hot-key traffic must be planned: one enormous room can concentrate load despite a large cluster. Model queries first, duplicate data deliberately for other access patterns, and document which constraints or joins the application must enforce. The category is not a universal guarantee about every implementation. || Partition: conversation ID / Cluster by message sequence / Read bounded sequence range / Hot conversation remains one pressure point

Graph storage makes relationships and traversals first-class, useful when variable-depth relationships are central to queries. Fraud investigation may traverse accounts, devices, and transfers. A relational schema can also represent edges, so compare actual query complexity and locality rather than assuming graph databases alone can model relationships. Bound traversal depth and fan-out, enforce tenant or privacy boundaries during expansion, and inspect the cost of high-degree nodes. An unrestricted traversal can become an expensive data-exfiltration path. || Start at account / Traverse permitted device edges / Bound depth and fan-out / Return evidence with authorization scope

Time-series data combines timestamps, measurements, and dimensions. Storage can exploit time locality, compression, partitioning, and retention. Device metrics with bounded labels fit this model, while adding a unique request ID as a metric label can explode cardinality. Decide handling for late data, clock skew, downsampling, and corrections. Time ordering by a device clock is not automatically causal ordering; keep ingestion time or sequence metadata when the distinction matters for investigation. || Device measurement timestamp / Ingest with source identity / Partition and retain by time / Downsample without hiding required detail

A search index stores representations optimized for retrieval rather than primary transactional ownership. An inverted index maps terms to postings; document scores and filters produce ranked results. Updates commonly propagate asynchronously from an authoritative store, so search may lag or temporarily retain deleted content. Authorization filtering must remain correct despite that lag. Keep enough source identity and version information to rebuild the index and reject stale updates; treating the index as the only copy makes recovery fragile. || Authoritative document version / Indexing transformation / Searchable postings and fields / Rebuild from authoritative source

A vector index supports similarity search over numerical embeddings. It typically combines a vector representation, an approximate candidate structure, and metadata filtering. Candidate recall, update cost, memory, distance function, and authorization affect the architecture. An embedding is a derived representation, not an authoritative replacement for a document. Preserve source versions and rebuild paths; approximate retrieval can miss relevant items, and a vector score does not prove a factual answer or permission to expose the source. || Source and embedding version / Candidate vector search / Metadata and permission filter / Fetch authoritative source evidence

Object storage exposes named blobs and metadata; block storage exposes addressable blocks; file storage exposes a filesystem namespace and file operations. Uploaded PDFs usually fit object storage plus relational metadata, while a database engine may need a block-backed filesystem with appropriate durability semantics. Protocol and implementation guarantees differ. A URL to an object is not the same as a committed metadata reference: incomplete uploads, orphan objects, and deletion require an explicit lifecycle protocol. || Client uploads object bytes / Object identity and checksum stored / Metadata commits reference / Cleanup handles abandoned objects

OLTP emphasizes many short transactions over current operational state; OLAP emphasizes analytical scans and aggregation over larger histories. Running an unbounded dashboard query on the purchase database can compete with checkout for I/O and memory. Replicating or transforming operational data into analytical storage separates access patterns but creates freshness, lineage, and reconciliation obligations. A single engine can support mixed workloads to a point; measure interference before adding another system solely because two labels exist. || Operational transaction commits / Data pipeline exports changes / Analytical layout serves aggregates / Freshness is explicitly bounded or observed

Row layouts keep fields of a record together; column layouts keep a field's values together across many records. Fetching one customer's complete operational record often favors row-oriented access, while summing revenue across millions of rows can benefit from reading only relevant columns and compression. Modern engines mix techniques, so compare the actual plan. Layout choice affects writes, updates, locality, and compression; it does not replace indexing or a sound logical model. || Row: fields for record A / Column: amounts across records / Point lookup versus aggregate scan / Choose by measured access pattern

Normalization avoids redundant facts and update anomalies; denormalization deliberately stores derived or repeated data to serve a read path. Store a customer's address once for their current profile, but retain the historical shipping address on a completed order as part of that order's fact. Not all duplication is an error. When a feed stores a cached author name, define whether it is a snapshot or a copy to maintain; ambiguity creates stale-data bugs. || One authoritative customer profile / Order retains historical address fact / Read model may duplicate display name / Each copy has an update policy

An ER model identifies entities, relationships, cardinalities, and constraints. Orders have many lines; each line refers to a product identity and stores purchase-time values needed historically. A diagram should distinguish mandatory from optional relationships and name keys. The model must address deletion and lifecycle: removing a product must not erase a completed order's financial meaning. A schema is an executable part of the design when its constraints enforce the intended relationships. || Customer owns many orders / Order owns many lines / Line references product identity / Historical values survive catalog changes

Referential integrity ensures a stored relationship refers to an allowed target under the chosen constraint. A foreign key can prevent an order line from referencing a missing order in a relational database. Cascading deletion is a policy decision, not automatically the right cleanup. Across separately owned services, a local foreign key cannot enforce remote existence; use explicit contracts, stable identifiers, events, and reconciliation. Application checks before insertion can race unless protected by an appropriate atomic mechanism. || Insert child reference / Database checks parent constraint / Atomic commit preserves local relation / Remote relation needs a different protocol

Data ownership names who may define and change a fact. Payments owns financial outcome records; analytics consumes a derived copy. If several services directly update one table, transaction behavior and release coupling become shared even if their code repositories are separate. Publish contracts for reading and changing owned data, and give consumers the least privilege required. Ownership includes retention, incident response, schema evolution, and recovery, not only the right to create a table. || Payment service owns outcome / Consumers use documented contract / Derived stores preserve source version / Owner manages correction and deletion

Choose storage by the operations and invariants it must support. Profiles may fit relational or document storage; financial entries benefit from transaction and audit constraints; chat history needs conversation/time access; event logs need ordered append and replay; PDFs need large immutable-byte handling. These are workload arguments, not mandatory product assignments. Prefer fewer systems until a measured access pattern justifies another. Every new engine adds backup, security, migration, telemetry, and operational expertise requirements. || Define query and write shapes / State required invariants / Estimate working set and growth / Select the smallest sufficient storage mix

Retention is an explicit lifecycle, not a scheduled delete statement added at the end. Decide legal and business purpose, expiration, archival, backup behavior, and derived-copy deletion. Dropping old partitions may be efficient for time-bounded data, while per-user deletion needs identity-aware propagation. A tombstone may prevent reappearance during replay but still retain personal data; minimize it appropriately. Verify that recovery from an old backup reapplies required deletion decisions instead of resurrecting removed records. || Record purpose and expiry / Delete or archive authoritative data / Propagate to derived stores / Reapply deletion after recovery

Polyglot persistence gives different workloads suitable engines at the cost of more operating boundaries. A shop might add search only after relational queries fail its relevance or latency needs. It then must track indexing lag, rebuilds, schemas, permissions, and recovery across both stores. Cross-store operations are not one transaction by default. Compare the added capability with total ownership cost, and keep authoritative data and derived representations distinguishable in every diagram and runbook. || Add specialized read engine / Define source of truth / Maintain and reconcile derived state / Pay operating and recovery cost''')

put(10, '''A database translates logical records into physical structures and coordinates access while preserving its promises. A lookup may consult an index, fetch pages into memory, inspect visibility, and return a row. The operating system and device still affect latency and durability. Understanding this path explains why one query may be CPU-bound while another waits on I/O. Start by measuring the plan and resource behavior; changing a database category without identifying the expensive step is guesswork. || Query reaches engine / Planner selects access path / Pages and versions are read / Visible records become results

A page is a fixed-size storage unit in many engines; records occupy pages; indexes point toward searchable values or records; a buffer pool caches useful pages. A cache hit avoids some storage I/O but still incurs parsing, execution, and synchronization. If a query touches a million scattered pages, extra application servers may only increase random reads. Working-set size and locality explain performance better than raw table row count alone. Exact page layouts and buffer policies are implementation-specific. || Record stored on page / Index locates candidate page / Buffer pool hit or disk read / Visibility check selects record version

B-tree families maintain ordered keys with bounded-height traversal; B+tree designs commonly keep data or record references in leaves linked for range scans. Hash indexing suits equality under its implementation but does not provide ordered range traversal. For keys [10,20,30,40], a range 20–35 benefits from an ordered path and neighboring leaves. Index choice depends on predicates, ordering, maintenance cost, and engine support. “An index is O(log n)” hides page I/O and result-size work. || Root chooses key range / Internal page narrows interval / Leaf locates key 20 / Neighboring leaf supplies range results

An LSM-based engine buffers writes, flushes sorted runs, and compacts them into a searchable organization. Sequential ingestion can help write-heavy workloads, while reads may consult several structures and compaction rewrites data. Write amplification counts physical bytes written relative to logical changes; it depends on layout and workload. Compaction consumes bandwidth and can cause stalls, so provision and observe it. A write-optimized name does not mean unlimited writes or free reads; compare tail latency under sustained ingestion. || Append durable intent / Update in-memory sorted state / Flush immutable sorted run / Compaction rewrites and merges runs

A query planner selects a physical plan from logical operations using statistics and cost estimates. EXPLAIN shows a proposed plan; execution analysis can reveal actual rows and time but may execute side effects depending on the command and engine. A join estimated at ten rows but producing a million can choose an unsuitable strategy. Inspect cardinality estimates, filters, scan types, sorts, and buffers. Update statistics or rewrite the query before blindly adding hardware or indexes. || SQL expresses result / Statistics estimate cardinality / Planner chooses joins and scans / Compare actual rows with estimates

An index speeds selected access paths while consuming space and write work. A composite index on (tenant_id,created_at,id) can support a tenant-scoped time-ordered page when the predicates and ordering fit. Unused or redundant indexes slow writes without helping reads. Covering an operation can avoid extra fetches in some engines, but larger indexes reduce cache locality. Test with representative data distribution and parameters; a small uniform fixture may conceal skew and poor selectivity. || Query filters tenant / Index orders time and ID / Read bounded matching range / Writes maintain index entries too

ACID separates atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability. Atomicity prevents partial transaction outcomes; consistency means the transaction preserves declared invariants when the application and constraints are correct; isolation governs concurrent observations; durability protects committed state under configuration and failure assumptions. The C is not a promise that all business rules are automatically inferred. A transaction that atomically transfers the wrong amount can satisfy engine mechanics while violating the intended business rule. || Begin transaction / Validate declared invariant / Apply related changes / Commit under isolation and durability contract

Commit establishes a transaction's accepted outcome according to the engine's protocol; rollback abandons its uncommitted work. Do not send a business success before commit completes if success requires persistence. Commit can fail or leave the client uncertain after a connection loss. Retrying the transaction may duplicate an external effect unless identity and coordination are explicit. Keep remote provider calls outside long-held local transactions where possible, and reconcile their results rather than pretend rollback reverses a remote charge. || Update local transaction / Request commit / Connection may fail around result / Reconcile uncertain outcome by identity

Write-ahead logging records recovery information before the corresponding dirty data pages are considered safely persisted. Recovery can use durable log records to reconstruct committed work even when data pages lag. Checkpointing limits recovery effort; it is not the same as a backup. Acknowledgment behavior depends on sync and replication configuration. Log durability alone cannot protect against loss of the only device containing both log and data, or an application committing logically wrong changes. || Change produces log record / Required log persistence precedes data / Commit point follows configured policy / Recovery replays appropriate logged work

MVCC maintains versions so readers can observe a defined snapshot while writers create new versions. Visibility depends on transaction rules, not simply the newest timestamp. A long-running reader can retain old versions and delay cleanup. Snapshot reads do not inherently prevent write skew: two transactions may read the same invariant and update different rows. Understand the engine's isolation level and conflict detection rather than treating “uses MVCC” as synonymous with serializable execution. || Reader snapshot established / Writer creates new row version / Reader sees its allowed version / Cleanup waits for relevant readers

Pessimistic locking coordinates access before conflicting work; optimistic concurrency validates that assumptions still hold before committing. A versioned update such as UPDATE seat SET owner=A,version=8 WHERE id=7 AND version=7 succeeds only if its expected version remains current. Zero affected rows means conflict, not success. Retrying must re-read and reconsider business conditions. Locks can wait or deadlock, while optimistic retries can waste work under contention; choose based on conflict rate and transaction cost. || Read seat version 7 / Compute proposed reservation / Conditional update expects version 7 / One winner advances version to 8

A dirty read observes uncommitted changes; a nonrepeatable read sees a previously read row change; a phantom changes the set matching a predicate. Starting with three open reservations, another transaction inserts a fourth before a repeated predicate query. The anomaly's occurrence depends on isolation and implementation. Do not assume that an isolation level's name implies identical behavior in every database. Define the problematic history and test the selected engine's documented guarantees. || Read matching reservation set / Concurrent transaction changes data / Repeat row or predicate read / Allowed result depends on isolation

Lost updates overwrite another writer's result; write skew violates a multi-row invariant after transactions update different rows; serialization anomalies are histories without an equivalent serial ordering. If two doctors each see the other on call and both mark themselves off, a snapshot can preserve each read yet violate “at least one on call.” Fix the invariant with appropriate serialization, locking, or a redesigned shared conflict point. Protecting only each individual row is insufficient. || A sees B on call / B sees A on call / A turns itself off / B turns itself off: invariant fails

Isolation levels specify allowed concurrency behavior, but engines implement them differently. PostgreSQL 18 Read Committed uses statement-level snapshots for ordinary reads; its Repeatable Read provides a transaction snapshot yet can permit serialization anomalies; Serializable adds conflict handling that can abort transactions. Other engines and statement forms differ. Applications must handle retryable failures by restarting the whole affected transaction with bounded attempts. Do not retry only the last statement while retaining assumptions from an invalidated snapshot. || Select documented engine and level / Construct anomaly history / Observe commit or conflict / Retry entire transaction when required

Serializable means the committed effect is equivalent to some serial transaction order, not necessarily wall-clock order. Linearizability is a distinct real-time single-operation property; strict serializability combines a transactional serial order with real-time constraints. A transaction may need retry after serialization failure. Keep external side effects out of blindly replayed transaction bodies or protect them with stable identity. Serialization makes concurrency reasoning stronger but does not validate incorrect business logic or solve every availability requirement. || Transactions execute concurrently / Engine rejects incompatible history / Committed effect has serial equivalent / Real-time order is a separate condition

A database pool caps active connections and queues acquisition. A larger pool can increase contention and memory rather than throughput. Measure connection hold time, lock waits, idle-in-transaction sessions, and queue age. In an illustrative pool of ten connections with 100 ms mean hold time, the ideal fully busy bound is 100 operations/s before other limits. If callers hold connections while waiting on external HTTP calls, shorten the ownership scope instead of only increasing the pool. || Borrow bounded connection / Execute short transaction / Release promptly / Observe acquisition and lock waits

A business operation spanning several statements needs one deliberate atomic boundary if its invariant requires all-or-nothing changes. Reserving inventory and recording a local reservation should commit together. An ORM save call is not proof that every related action shares the transaction. Across separate stores or external services, use an explicit distributed protocol or workflow with reconciliation. Transaction scope should be as small as possible while preserving the invariant, and must not leak through lazy results after disposal. || Decrement available inventory / Insert reservation record / Commit both local changes / Failure rolls back the local unit

Backups provide recoverable historical state; replication keeps live copies and can replicate mistakes. Test restores into a usable environment and verify application invariants, credentials, schema versions, and recovery time. A backup job reporting success does not prove that its archive is complete or decryptable. Specify point-in-time recovery requirements and how logs are retained. Durability is conditional on acknowledged persistence and independent failure protection, not established by merely owning a backup file. || Capture consistent backup / Retain required recovery logs / Restore into clean environment / Verify data and recovery objectives''')

put(11, '''Caching works when requests reuse data or nearby data often enough to avoid more expensive work. A product description read thousands of times between edits is a candidate; a unique one-use computation may not be. Compare hit latency, miss cost, maintenance, and correctness. With assumed 1 ms hits, 50 ms misses, and 90% hits, the simple weighted service-time average is 5.9 ms, excluding queues and other work. A hit ratio alone does not describe tail latency or cost. || Repeated product lookup / Hit returns cached representation / Miss reaches authoritative store / Benefit depends on reuse and miss cost

Temporal locality is reuse over time; spatial locality is reuse of nearby data. The working set is the data actively needed over a relevant interval. A cache too small for that set may churn even if total historical data is enormous. Scan traffic can evict useful entries under some policies. Measure bytes, object sizes, skew, and admission behavior, not only key count. Caching every object eagerly can waste memory on cold data while crowding out hot content. || Hot working set / Bounded cache memory / Admission and eviction decisions / Observe churn and useful hit rate

An in-process cache is fast and simple but creates independent copies per application instance. A distributed cache shares a network-accessible state layer, adding serialization, connection, and availability costs. If two app instances cache price 10 and 12, reads differ until policy resolves freshness. Do not place an invariant such as inventory allocation solely in an ordinary stale cache. Choose the location by reuse, memory duplication, required coordination, and failure behavior. || App A caches price 10 / Database commits price 12 / App B sees refreshed value / App A may remain stale

Browser, CDN, application, and database caches sit at different ownership and trust boundaries. A personalized response cached at a public edge without the right key can leak data. A database buffer cache stores pages, while an application cache stores computed representations; invalidating one does not invalidate the other. Draw every copy and define freshness at each layer. Diagnosing a stale response requires knowing which layer answered and which version it held. || Browser representation / Shared edge representation / Application object copy / Database page and committed version

Cache-aside loads after a miss; read-through delegates loading to the cache interface; write-through updates a backing store in the write path; write-behind defers backing persistence. Name the actual ordering and failure semantics because implementations vary. In write-behind, acknowledging only a volatile cache can lose accepted changes. Cache-aside still has races: a delayed reader can populate an old value after a newer write invalidated the key. Use versioning or stronger coordination when freshness requires it. || Cache miss reads old value / Writer commits new value / Writer invalidates cache / Delayed reader can repopulate old value

A TTL bounds intended residence after insertion, not necessarily the age of the underlying fact when inserted. A cached value already five minutes old with a one-minute TTL can remain six minutes behind its source. Expiration is distinct from eviction, and clients may retain their own copies. Use jitter to avoid synchronized expirations. Choose TTL from tolerated staleness and refresh behavior; correctness-critical actions should validate authoritative state rather than trust a display cache. || Value observed at source time T / Cached later at T plus delay / TTL begins at insertion / Source age includes both delay and TTL

Invalidation coordinates derived copies with authoritative changes. Delete-after-write is common but cannot by itself prevent every stale repopulation race. Versioned cache entries, conditional population, or source-version checks can strengthen semantics. Events for invalidation can lag or repeat, so observe version and propagation time. For prices, stale display may be acceptable while checkout revalidates the current price; for inventory, accepting stale availability as authority can oversell. Match the cache contract to the operation's invariant. || Authoritative version increases / Invalidation or update is emitted / Cached copy compares version / Critical operation validates authoritative state

LRU favors recent access; LFU favors frequent access; FIFO evicts insertion order. Their precise implementations may be approximate. For capacity three and accesses A,B,C,A,D, ideal LRU evicts B; FIFO evicts A; a frequency-based policy also needs tie-breaking. Compare policies with representative traffic and object sizes. A clever eviction algorithm cannot fix a working set vastly beyond memory or a cache that stores low-value objects indiscriminately. Admission and eviction are separate choices. || Capacity: 3 entries / Access A, B, C, A / Insert D / LRU removes B; FIFO removes A

A hot key concentrates traffic on one shard or owner. More cache nodes do not distribute a single key automatically. Options include local read copies, replication where supported, request coalescing, or deliberate partitioning of commutative aggregates. Each affects freshness or aggregation cost. For a stock count, blindly splitting into independent counters can violate allocation correctness. First identify whether the pressure is read bandwidth, write serialization, object size, or repeated recomputation. || One product becomes viral / One cache owner receives most reads / Add suitable read copies or coalescing / Preserve authoritative allocation semantics

A stampede occurs when many misses trigger duplicate expensive work. Penetration repeatedly requests absent keys; an avalanche is a broad synchronized loss or expiry of cached entries. Use single-flight loading, bounded refresh workers, negative caching, TTL jitter, and origin admission limits as appropriate. Locks used for refresh also need expiration and failure handling. A cache outage should not let every application instance independently overwhelm the database while attempting to reconstruct the entire working set. || Many readers miss at once / Duplicate origin queries accumulate / Coalesce refresh for one key / Bound total origin work during recovery

Negative caching stores an absence or error under a narrow policy. Caching “product not found” for a short interval can protect against repeated nonexistent lookups. Do not cache a temporary database failure as permanent absence, and do not let one tenant's authorization failure poison another tenant's key. A newly created product may remain hidden until negative-entry expiry or invalidation. Use separate error types, short appropriate lifetimes, and correctly scoped keys. || Product P absent / Cache absence for bounded interval / Product P is created / Invalidate absence or wait defined TTL

Stale-while-revalidate serves an allowed stale value while one refresh updates it. This trades freshness for latency and origin protection; it is not suitable for every operation. A cached description can be stale, while payment authorization cannot be inferred from an old response. Define fresh, stale-allowed, and unusable age limits. If refresh repeatedly fails, do not extend stale serving forever unless that is an explicit degradation contract visible to users and operators. || Value becomes stale / Reader receives permitted old copy / One background refresh runs / Hard age limit eventually forbids serving

Warming preloads likely useful entries; prefetching predicts near-future requests. Both consume bandwidth and may evict more valuable content. Warm a measured working set gradually after deployment rather than issuing a full-database scan from every new instance. Track whether prefetched entries are actually used and cancel obsolete work. A cold-start problem may be better addressed through staggered rollout, shared cache layers, or request admission than an increasingly elaborate prediction system. || New instance starts / Warm bounded hot set / Observe useful prefetch ratio / Avoid coordinated origin overload

Redis-like structures include strings, hashes, lists, sets, sorted sets, and streams, each with different operations and cost models. A sorted set can track a time-ordered window; a set can deduplicate identifiers within a lifecycle. Atomicity of one command is not automatically atomicity of a multi-command workflow. Server-side scripts or transactions may provide the needed local boundary, but durability, replication, cluster key placement, and expiry still matter. Do not assume every structure is a durable queue by default. || Select structure for operation / Keep compound invariant atomic / Bound memory and expiry / Check persistence and cluster semantics

An edge cache serves representations near clients, while an origin shield can consolidate misses before the origin. Signed URLs and cache keys need policy: query parameters, headers, tenant identity, and content versions can change what is safe to share. A purge request propagates under the provider's contract and may not invalidate every client-held copy. Use immutable versioned asset URLs when practical. Delivery improves bandwidth and latency for suitable content but does not authorize serving private data globally. || Client to nearby edge / Edge miss reaches shield / Shield miss reaches origin / Versioned content separates representations

Partitioning distributes keys; replication creates copies for resilience or reads under a protocol. A cache may lose or evict entries during failover, and replicas may lag. The application must decide whether to fall back, serve allowed stale data, or reject. Consistent hashing reduces some remapping during membership changes but cannot prevent hot-key skew. Keep cache and authoritative-store failure domains and recovery capacity explicit; rebuilding after a cluster event can be the real peak workload. || Key maps to partition / Partition may have replicas / Failover can expose misses or lag / Origin recovery budget remains bounded

Caching is harmful when maintenance and correctness cost exceed saved work. A low-reuse result cache may consume memory and serialization time without many hits. A cache with missing authorization scope can leak data; one that masks a slow database during normal operation can hide an origin capacity deficit until outage. Test the system with the cache empty or unavailable. Prefer query/index improvements or simpler data access when they address the bottleneck with fewer state copies. || Measure saved origin work / Count invalidation and operating cost / Test cold and failed cache / Remove cache when it worsens the contract''')

put(12, '''Vertical scaling increases resources of one node; horizontal scaling adds nodes. Neither guarantees proportional throughput. A larger database host may buy time without distributed data management, while extra stateless application instances can distribute independent compute. Shared locks, disks, or downstream services can remain limiting. Compare failure impact, scaling delay, cost, and operational complexity. Choose the smallest change supported by profiling, then rerun the workload because the limiting resource can move. || Identify saturated resource / Increase node capacity or add owners / Shared bottleneck may remain / Measure completed useful work again

A stateless service retains no required per-client conversational state locally between requests; it still operates on state elsewhere. This makes instance replacement and traffic distribution easier. A server-local session or uncommitted job can prevent transparent failover. Move required state to an appropriate owner, or make affinity and recovery explicit. Sending every request to any instance is safe only if identity, state access, configuration, and side-effect handling are compatible across those instances. || Request reaches instance A / Required state has external owner / Next request reaches instance B / B reconstructs context under contract

Round-robin spreads selections, least-connections approximates active load, weighted policies reflect capacity, and hashing can preserve affinity. Their effectiveness depends on request cost and connection behavior. One expensive request can occupy a backend much longer than many cheap ones. Observe queueing and per-instance saturation, not only request counts. Client-side or server-side balancing also affects discovery, retries, and failure detection. A distribution algorithm cannot create capacity at a saturated shared database. || Choose backend by policy / Observe actual work and queue / Avoid unhealthy or overloaded target / Rebalance without multiplying retries

Health checks approximate readiness, not omniscient correctness. Liveness asks whether a process should be restarted; readiness asks whether it should receive traffic under the chosen policy. A check that requires every downstream service can remove all instances during a shared dependency outage and worsen recovery. Use meaningful local signals, bounded checks, and deliberate degradation policy. Failover also requires state and connection behavior that support the switch; routing alone does not recover uncommitted work. || Probe readiness / Remove target when policy says unsafe / Route new traffic elsewhere / State recovery remains a separate obligation

Sticky sessions keep a client associated with an instance and can support local state or reduce cache misses. They also create uneven load and complicate failover. A user's connection may outlive the instance, so required state needs recovery or an explicit reconnection protocol. Prefer external session ownership for simple replaceable web instances when feasible. Affinity is an optimization or a deliberate constraint, not a substitute for deciding where authoritative state lives. || Client assigned instance A / Later requests retain affinity / A fails or drains / Recover session or reconnect explicitly

Autoscaling reacts to a measured signal, with delay and limits. CPU may suit compute-heavy workers, queue age may better represent background latency, and connection saturation may expose an I/O bottleneck. Scaling on request count alone assumes similar cost per request. Use stabilization and maximums to avoid oscillation or runaway spend. Provision startup headroom and admission control so a sudden spike does not exhaust the system before new capacity becomes ready. || Metric crosses threshold / Controller requests capacity / Instances start and warm / Admission limits protect the delay

Replication maintains copies under an acknowledgment and reconciliation protocol. It can improve availability or read distribution, but may introduce lag, conflict, and acknowledged-write-loss risks depending on configuration. Async replication can acknowledge before a replica has the write; sync replication can increase latency or block during failure. State which replicas are required for acknowledgment and which failures promotion tolerates. A second copy is not a proof of durability without independent failure domains and recovery rules. || Leader accepts write / Protocol determines required acknowledgments / Replica may lag / Promotion must honor the durability contract

Partitioning divides a dataset; sharding commonly distributes those partitions across separate storage owners. Choose a key that supports important queries and distributes work. Sharding by tenant can isolate some traffic but leave one large tenant hot. Cross-shard joins, uniqueness, transactions, and migrations become more complex. Keep a routing model and relocation procedure. Add partitioning when a measured storage or write bottleneck justifies it, rather than assuming every large user count requires it. || Choose ownership key / Route records to partition / Query may cross owners / Rebalance with explicit movement protocol

Range partitioning preserves ordered locality and can make time-based retention efficient, but a newest range may receive most writes. Hash partitioning spreads keys more evenly while making ordered scans and range locality harder. Neither avoids skew if one key dominates. For event ingestion, partitioning only by current day concentrates all writers; combining a time bucket with a spread dimension can help at the cost of multi-partition reads. Define the resulting query fan-out. || Range: related keys together / Hash: keys spread by function / Hot key remains hot / Query locality and write balance trade off

Consistent hashing places keys and owners in a mapping that changes only part of the assignment when membership changes; virtual nodes can improve distribution. Rendezvous hashing scores each key-owner pair and chooses the highest eligible score, offering a different implementation of stable assignment. Neither guarantees equal traffic because request popularity is not uniform. Membership agreement, migration, replication, and hot-key handling remain required. Avoid changing hash functions without a plan for old data and client routing. || Stable owner set / Map key using ring or scores / Add owner and remap subset / Transfer data and preserve request routing

Rebalancing moves data or ownership while serving traffic. A robust move copies a snapshot, captures concurrent changes, validates catch-up, and changes routing under an ownership epoch or equivalent protocol. Readers and writers must not disagree indefinitely about the owner. Throttle movement so it does not consume all production I/O. Keep a rollback and reconciliation path; deleting the old copy before the new owner is authoritative can turn a routine capacity change into data loss. || Copy partition snapshot / Apply concurrent changes / Switch ownership epoch / Retire old copy after validation

Hot partitions arise from uneven key popularity, data size, or write concentration. A heatmap of requests and bytes per partition reveals skew hidden by fleet averages. Splitting a range may help if traffic divides; a single hot entity may need read copies, batching, or a redesigned invariant owner. Randomizing writes can distribute append pressure but makes reads aggregate more partitions. Match the fix to the actual pressure rather than repeatedly adding cold capacity. || Fleet average appears healthy / One partition receives 70% of writes / Its queue determines tail latency / Split or redesign the specific hot path

Read replicas can distribute suitable reads while the primary retains writes. A read immediately after an update can observe stale data if routed to a lagging replica. Route critical reads to a sufficiently current owner, use session position tracking where supported, or accept an explicit freshness contract. Read/write splitting must classify operations by required semantics, not only by SQL verb: a SELECT may participate in a consistency-critical transaction or require a lock. || Write commits at primary / Replica has older log position / Replica read can be stale / Critical read uses sufficient freshness

Replication lag is the distance between source and replica state, measured by time, positions, or application version. A small average lag can hide a replica stalled for a particular workload. Expose source progress and replica apply progress, and distinguish transport lag from apply lag. Users may need read-your-writes even when global reads tolerate eventual freshness. Failover decisions must account for lag and acknowledged-write policy; the fastest promotion is not necessarily the safest promotion. || Source commits position 100 / Replica applied position 95 / Read may miss positions 96–100 / Promotion and routing inspect progress

A database proxy can pool, route, or multiplex connections according to implementation. It does not remove database transaction limits and can change session-state assumptions. A client using session variables, temporary tables, or long transactions may not be safe under arbitrary multiplexing. Document where authentication, routing, and failover decisions happen. The proxy itself needs capacity and failure handling; placing it in a diagram does not eliminate connection saturation or guarantee transparent retries. || Client borrows proxy connection / Proxy selects backend session / Transaction pins required state / Release only when session contract allows

When a pool is full, waiting callers accumulate outside the database as well as inside it. More application replicas can multiply total database connections beyond the safe limit. Bound the aggregate across instances, reserve recovery capacity, and include acquisition time in deadlines. A query waiting on a lock may hold its connection while doing no CPU work. Fix hold time, contention, and admission before expanding every pool and increasing the pressure that caused the queue. || App instances multiply pools / Database connection budget is fixed / Borrowers wait and time out / Bound aggregate pressure and shorten ownership

Bottleneck diagnosis follows the critical path and resource saturation. Compare offered load, good completions, queues, CPU, memory, disk, network, connection waits, and lock contention. If a single database query dominates, caching or indexing may help more than extra web instances; if CPU is saturated with independent work, compute scaling may help. Change one material variable and measure again. A component's high utilization is a clue, not proof that it limits the business outcome. || Trace request critical path / Inspect resource and queue evidence / Change demonstrated limiting stage / Re-measure useful throughput and tails

A queue absorbs temporary mismatch, not sustained overload. If arrivals exceed completion, the queue grows until latency, memory, or a limit fails. Bound queue size and age, reject or shed excess work, and prioritize essential operations. Waiting longer can make a request useless while still consuming resources. With a two-second deadline, a ten-second backlog should not be silently accepted as if work were on time. Return an explicit overload outcome and avoid automatic retry storms. || Arrival exceeds service rate / Waiting time grows / Deadline usefulness expires / Bound admission before resources exhaust

A single point of failure can be a machine, database, credentials service, router, operator action, or shared dependency. Count independent failure domains rather than only process replicas. Two application containers on one host do not survive that host's loss. Redundancy also needs failover, capacity, detection, and state correctness. Inspect control-plane dependencies during recovery: the data path may be redundant while a missing configuration service prevents every replacement instance from starting. || Two replicas share one host / Host fails / Both replicas disappear / Separate correlated failure domains deliberately

Multi-region designs trade latency, data placement, conflict, cost, and failure behavior. Active-passive can simplify write ownership but requires tested promotion and recovery targets. Active-active may improve locality or continuity but needs a precise conflict or coordination model. Cross-region acknowledgment adds propagation delay; asynchronous copies can lose recent acknowledged writes on failover. Start with business requirements for locality, tolerated interruption, and data loss, then choose write authority and read contracts per operation. || Select regional write ownership / Define acknowledgment across regions / Partition changes allowed behavior / Failover preserves stated recovery contract

Read scaling often replicates or caches immutable or sufficiently stale-tolerant data. Write scaling must distribute, batch, or reduce ownership work while preserving invariants. A globally unique sequential allocator or one hot account can remain serialized across many shards. Reduce unnecessary indexes or coordination before distributing more components. Aggregate counters may allow commutative updates, while seat allocation needs exclusive ownership. Different business operations can legitimately use different scaling and consistency strategies. || Reads reuse representations / Writes alter authoritative state / Partition or batch suitable writes / Preserve the actual invariant boundary

Scale-out economics includes duplicate baseline capacity, coordination, networking, telemetry, cache warmup, and operational work. Ten small nodes are not automatically cheaper or faster than one larger node. Compare equal service objectives and failure headroom under the same workload; include the capacity required during a node or zone loss. Managed systems can trade provider charges against staffing and recovery work. Use cost per successful business outcome and investigate bottlenecks before purchasing capacity that cannot help. || Same workload and objectives / Include failure and migration headroom / Count network and operating costs / Compare cost per successful outcome''')

put(13, '''Store large binary content separately from mutable processing metadata when their access and lifecycle needs differ. An object has a stable identity, content version or checksum, size, and ownership metadata; a job record references it. A database row pointing at a missing or incomplete object is a broken workflow even if both services individually succeed. Use explicit upload states and cleanup for abandoned bytes. Treat object-store-specific consistency and durability claims as implementation contracts to verify, not category-wide assumptions. || Upload object bytes / Verify object identity and size / Commit metadata reference / Process only verified complete input

A large-file architecture can issue a scoped upload authorization so the client streams directly to storage, while the application owns metadata and authorization. This avoids proxying all bytes through web workers, but the client may never complete the upload. Validate ownership, quotas, content size, and completion evidence before making a file available for processing. Expire abandoned intents and reconcile unreferenced objects. A signed upload URL is a time-bounded capability that must not grant arbitrary bucket access. || Create upload intent / Client sends bytes to storage / Server validates completion / Durable job becomes ready

Multipart upload splits a file into identifiable parts with explicit completion. Resumability tracks confirmed offsets or parts so a client can retry without restarting all bytes. Duplicate part submission and changed source content need defined behavior. Verify the completed object rather than assuming every acknowledged part implies a correct final file. Multipart identifiers and checksums can be provider-specific; a convenient entity tag is not universally a content hash. Clean up incomplete sessions to prevent silent storage growth. || Upload intent and parts manifest / Confirm parts 1, 2 and 4 / Retry missing part 3 / Complete and verify assembled object

A checksum detects accidental corruption under its algorithm's properties; a cryptographic digest can identify content with collision assumptions and appropriate verification. Content addressing uses a digest-derived identity, enabling deduplication or immutable references. It does not authorize access or prove the content is safe. Scope deduplication carefully across tenants to avoid information leakage, and verify downloaded bytes when integrity matters. A hash of a client-provided claim is not a checksum of the bytes actually stored. || Hash actual uploaded bytes / Compare expected digest / Store immutable content identity / Authorize every reference independently

Binary bytes and metadata have different update patterns. Keep file identity and immutable byte versions distinct from classification status, user annotations, and processing attempts. An extractor should record exactly which content and model version produced an output. Mutating a file in place under the same identifier can make old results impossible to explain. If the input changes, create a new version and schedule appropriate reprocessing while retaining or deleting historical artifacts according to policy. || Immutable file version F1 / Metadata points to F1 / Extraction output records F1 and model M / New bytes become F2

An inverted index maps a term to postings describing documents or positions containing it. For documents D1='blue car' and D2='blue bike', blue maps to D1,D2 while car maps only to D1. Analysis choices such as tokenization and normalization affect matching. Search updates need document identity and version to prevent an old extraction from replacing a newer one. Keep source records rebuildable; the index is a derived serving structure with its own freshness and permission obligations. || D1: blue car / D2: blue bike / blue maps to D1 and D2 / car maps only to D1

An indexing pipeline transforms authoritative content into searchable representations. Separate extraction, validation, enrichment, indexing, and publication states so a partial failure is diagnosable. Retry a stage using stable input and output identities; avoid creating duplicate documents on every retry. Poison files need bounded attempts and a review path. Check that index permissions derive from current ownership rather than stale public flags embedded in an old output. Completion should refer to a precise index version and source version. || Source version ready / Extract and validate text / Index with stable document ID / Publish searchable state and version

Reindexing builds a new representation while preserving serving behavior. Take a snapshot or defined source position, process its records, catch up subsequent changes, validate, and switch a serving alias or routing epoch. Do not overwrite the only working index before the new one is verified. Deletions and updates during the build need replay or reconciliation. Rollback must preserve schema compatibility and any new writes; counting indexed documents alone cannot establish that the right versions or permissions were indexed. || Build index generation B / Replay changes after snapshot / Validate versions and deletions / Switch serving pointer from A to B

Batch processing operates on bounded sets; stream processing operates on continuing input with explicit progress and state. A nightly export may be simpler when hours of freshness delay are acceptable; fraud signals may require lower-latency updates. Streaming introduces late data, state recovery, partitioning, and checkpoint concerns. Batch can still be expensive and failure-prone. Choose based on freshness and recomputation needs, and specify whether results are provisional or final when late events can change them. || Bounded daily batch / Continuous event stream / Late events may revise output / Progress and recovery need explicit state

ETL transforms before loading into the target; ELT loads and transforms in the target environment. ETL can minimize sensitive fields before they reach an analytical store; ELT can preserve flexible raw inputs but increases governance and storage obligations. Either can lose lineage or silently change semantics if transformations are undocumented. Version transformations, validate schemas, and define ownership of raw and derived datasets. The acronym does not determine quality, privacy, or repeatability. || Extract source / Transform before load: ETL / Load before transform: ELT / Version and govern both paths

Change Data Capture emits committed changes from a source through a supported mechanism such as a transaction log. It can keep search or analytics current without polling entire tables. A snapshot plus a change stream needs a consistent handoff position so writes are neither missed nor applied incorrectly. Events can repeat, and schema changes affect decoding. Preserve source identity and ordering metadata, deduplicate, and monitor lag. CDC does not automatically make the destination transactionally consistent with the source for every query. || Source snapshot position recorded / Committed changes stream / Destination applies stable versions / Handoff prevents gaps and duplicate effects

A data lake stores flexible datasets, often in object storage; a warehouse provides curated analytical structures and serving capabilities. Boundaries vary by product. Raw retention can help replay but also accumulates privacy, quality, and discovery problems. A lake without schema, ownership, and lineage becomes difficult to trust. Model immutable inputs, curated outputs, catalog metadata, access policies, and lifecycle separately. Choose formats and partitioning for the queries and recovery operations that actually need to run. || Raw versioned dataset / Validated curated transformation / Analytical serving layout / Catalog and retention cover every stage

Stream processing maintains results over ongoing events using windows, keys, and state. Event time describes when a source event occurred; processing time describes when it was handled. Watermarks estimate progress under assumptions about late arrivals; they are not proof that no earlier event can ever arrive. Define allowed lateness, correction behavior, checkpoint ownership, and effect boundaries. A windowed count can be updated after a late event, so consumers need to understand whether it is provisional or final. || Event occurs at 10:00 / Processor receives at 10:03 / Window policy handles late data / Checkpoint restores processing state

Provenance records where an artifact came from; lineage connects transformations between artifacts. An extracted amount should link to file version, page or region, extractor version, parameters, and review decisions. This supports debugging and correction without claiming the extraction is always right. Store stable references, not only a text explanation that cannot be checked. Privacy and retention apply to provenance too; an audit trail containing raw personal data can outlive the source if lifecycle policies are incomplete. || File F version 2 / Extractor M version 5 / Field derived from page 3 / Human review and downstream output linked

Data quality checks validate shape, ranges, identity, relationships, and completeness against explicit contracts. Schema-valid records can still be semantically wrong: a currency field may be present but incompatible with the amount units. Quarantine invalid inputs with reasons and preserve enough evidence for repair. Replay must be versioned and deduplicated so fixing a transformation does not double side effects. Compare counts, versions, and business invariants at source and sink rather than trusting an empty error queue. || Validate input schema / Check semantic invariant / Quarantine invalid record / Replay repaired version without duplicate effect

Lifecycle policies should follow every copy: raw files, extracted text, thumbnails, indexes, logs, and backups. A retention timer on the original PDF alone leaves derived personal data behind. Use deletion workflows with durable progress and reconciliation; maintain narrowly scoped tombstones where required to prevent replay resurrection. Large object collections need efficient listing or ownership indexes for deletion. Test recovery from old snapshots against deletion history so a restore does not silently reverse required privacy actions. || Deletion intent recorded / Raw and derived artifacts removed / Index and caches updated / Recovery reapplies deletion decisions

A data-intensive design is a network of state transformations, not a collection of product logos. For every edge, define source authority, version, ordering, retry identity, validation, ownership, and recovery. Keep immutable source versions so derived structures can be rebuilt, but do not retain everything indefinitely without purpose. End-to-end reliability comes from coordinating those contracts and observing progress across the pipeline. A scalable queue cannot compensate for an extractor that loses provenance or an index that serves unauthorized copies. || Authoritative versioned input / Validated deterministic transformations / Durable processing and effect identities / Reconciled searchable authorized output''')
